## Case: context-manager-close
id: "av.parity.1094.context-manager-close"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1094"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.as"
syntax: "auto-script"

```auto
use.py io: StringIO
use.py contextlib: closing
fn main() int {
 let buf = StringIO()
 try { with closing(buf) as r { r.write("ok"); fail("stop") } } catch e { }
 if buf.closed.to(bool) { return 42 }; return 0
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "foreign.scalar", "foreign.resource", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
foreign: { kind: "rust", provider: "py" }
exit: 42

#### stdout

```text
```

#### stderr

```text
```

### Notes

`with closing(buf) as r` enters real CPython context managers; the body
raises a language error (`fail`), `__exit__` runs EXACTLY ONCE and closes
`buf` (the `buf.closed` truthiness is the parent-side close oracle), and the
error propagates to the outer catch (`closing` does not suppress).

